{"id": "668", "question": "what happens when you breath in and out", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is what happens when you breathe in and out:\n\nWhen you breathe in (inhalation):\n\n* The intercostal muscles contract, causing the ribcage to expand.\n* The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest.\n* Pressure inside the chest is lowered, allowing air to be sucked into the lungs.\n\nWhen you breathe out (exhalation):\n\n* The diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity.\n* The intercostal muscles relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity.\n* Air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of the lungs and windpipe, and then out of the nose or mouth.\n\nSo, in summary, when you breathe in, the diaphragm and intercostal muscles contract to bring air into the lungs, and when you breathe out, the diaphragm and intercostal muscles relax to force air out of the lungs.", "pred": "When you breathe in and out, your diaphragm contracts and relaxes. This movement causes the air in your lungs to expand and contract. As you inhale, the diaphragm moves downward, increasing the volume of your chest cavity, which creates negative pressure. This negative pressure causes air to flow into your lungs. Conversely, when you exhale, your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward, decreasing the volume of your chest cavity, which creates positive pressure. This positive pressure forces air out of your lungs. The process of breathing in and out is continuous and helps to exchange oxygen and carbon dioxide in your body.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_pd", "raw_output": "Answer: When you breathe in and out, your diaphragm contracts and relaxes. This movement causes the air in your lungs to expand and contract. As you inhale, the diaphragm moves downward, increasing the volume of your chest cavity, which creates negative pressure. This negative pressure causes air to flow into your lungs. Conversely, when you exhale, your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward, decreasing the volume of your chest cavity, which creates positive pressure. This positive pressure forces air out of your lungs. The process of breathing in and out is continuous and helps to exchange oxygen and carbon dioxide in your body.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nPlease generate the NEXT logical reasoning step. Do NOT provide the final answer yet. Focus strictly on analyzing the physical details step by step.\nAnswer: When you breathe in and out, your diaphragm contracts and relaxes. This movement causes the air in your lungs to expand and contract. As you inhale, the diaphragm moves downward, increasing the volume of your chest cavity, which creates negative pressure. This negative pressure causes air to flow into your lungs. Conversely, when you exhale, your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward, decreasing the volume of your chest cavity, which creates positive pressure. This positive pressure\nPlease generate the NEXT logical reasoning step. Do NOT provide the final answer yet. Focus strictly on analyzing the physical details step by step.\nAnswer: When you breathe in and out, your diaphragm contracts and relaxes. This movement causes the air in your lungs to expand and contract. As you inhale, the diaphragm moves downward, increasing the volume of your chest cavity, which creates negative pressure. This negative pressure causes air to flow into your lungs. Conversely, when you exhale, your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward, decreasing the volume of your chest cavity, which creates positive pressure. This positive pressure\nThe reasoning steps are:\n\nAnswer: When you breathe in and out, your diaphragm contracts and relaxes. This movement causes the air in your lungs to expand and contract. As you inhale, the diaphragm moves downward, increasing the volume of your chest cavity, which creates negative pressure. This negative pressure causes air to flow into your lungs. Conversely, when you exhale, your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward, decreasing the volume of your chest cavity, which creates positive pressure. This positive pressure forces air out of your lungs. The process of breathing in and out is continuous and helps to exchange oxygen and carbon dioxide in your body.", "time_sec": 27.641, "generation_time_seconds": 27.5973, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0354, "record_process": true, "stages": [{"step": 0, "phase": "rollout", "rollouts": [{"beam": 0, "rollout": 0, "token_ids": [198, 785, 32711, 7354, 525, 24391, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0, -1.0143, -0.0481, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.1251, -8.1593, -14.48, -11.0419, -3.9473, -5.8035, -8.194, -7.5986, -8.5771, -5.4787, -2.3963, -1.9824, -1.9093, -2.0204, -1.5796, -0.7054, -0.4673, -0.9082, -0.8874, -1.9248, -3.2031, -2.9835, -1.8477, -0.51, -0.3653, -2.2097, -3.9816, -6.0472, -6.0478, -2.205, -1.3518, -1.2034, -2.5856, -3.5739, -5.4247, -2.7781, -1.0624, -0.2602, -0.5135, -0.808, -2.5854, -3.5769, -2.9747, -2.2071, -2.5855, -3.777, -3.7756, 