{"id": "492", "question": "how can you tell who liked your tweet", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, there are several ways to track who likes your tweets:\n\n1. Favotter.net and Favstar.fm - These websites allow you to track who has favorited your tweets and which tweets have been favorited by multiple users. (Passage 1)\n2. Using a web-based website - There are websites that allow you to check Twitter users who favorite tweets. (Passage 2)\n3. On Twitter - You can set up your account settings to receive notifications via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as a favorite. (Passage 3)\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question is: You can use Favotter.net, Favstar.fm, or a web-based website to track who likes your tweets, or you can set up your Twitter account settings to receive email notifications.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_pd", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nTo determine how to identify who liked a tweet, the logical reasoning steps should focus on the features or methods provided by the platform or service where the tweet was posted. For example, if the platform has a feature that shows the names of people who liked the tweet, then you can check that list. If the platform does not provide such a feature, then you may need to rely on other methods such as asking the person who posted the tweet or using a third-party tool or service that tracks likes. However, without specific information about the platform or service, it is not possible to provide a definitive answer. Therefore, the answer is:\nTo determine how to identify who liked a tweet, the logical reasoning steps should focus on the features or methods provided by the platform or service where the tweet was posted. For example, if the platform has a feature that shows the names of people who liked the tweet, then you can check that list. If the platform does not provide such a feature, then you may need to rely on other methods such as asking the person who posted the tweet or using a third-party tool or service that tracks likes. However, without specific information about the platform or service, it is not possible to provide a definitive answer. Therefore, the answer is:\nThe reasoning steps are:\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 19.568, "generation_time_seconds": 19.5011, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0547, "record_process": true, "stages": [{"step": 0, "phase": "rollout", "rollouts": [{"beam": 0, "rollout": 0, "token_ids": [198, 1249, 8253, 1246, 311, 10542, 879, 14915, 264, 11809, 11, 279, 19819, 32711, 7354, 1265, 5244, 389, 279, 4419, 476, 5413, 3897, 553, 279, 5339, 476, 2473, 1380, 279, 11809, 572, 8454, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 421, 279, 5339, 702, 264, 4565, 429, 4933, 279, 5036, 315, 1251, 879, 14915, 279, 11809, 11, 1221, 498, 646, 1779, 429, 1140, 13, 1416, 279, 5339, 1558, 537, 3410, 1741, 264, 4565, 11, 1221, 498, 1231, 1184, 311, 17188, 389, 1008, 5413, 1741, 438, 10161, 279, 1697, 879, 8454, 279, 11809, 476, 1667, 264, 4843, 24031, 5392, 476, 2473, 429, 13918, 13151, 13, 4354, 11, 2041, 3151, 1995, 911, 279, 5339, 476, 2473, 11, 432, 374, 537, 3204, 311, 3410, 264, 44713, 4226, 13, 15277, 11, 279, 4226, 374, 25], "logp_list": [-0.0, -1.8095, -0.0755, -1.2367, -1.0598, -4.5944, -0.0102, -0.0002, -2.2007, -0.0203, -0.0183, -2.3671, -1.1142, -1.6828, -0.5984, -0.8105, -1.366, -0.0, -1.3446, -1.7192, -1.5733, -1.6234, -0.3339, -0.3451, -0.0597, -0.1224, -0.8932, -0.7558, -0.9929, -0.0005, -0.0055, -0.0201, -0.0026, -0.0115, -1.3358, -0.1055, -0.0241, -0.0754, -0.0133, -2.2032, -0.6307, -0.0314, -0.0641, -0.112, -0.432, -1.0358, -1.4577, -0.0793, -2.1618, -0.0, -0.0696, -1.0581, -0.1174, -0.0029, -1.6376, -1.1837, -0.1937, -2.0898, -0.7675, -2.2901, -0.7101, -0.1494, -0.2886, -0.0024, -0.1063, -0.0, -0.5186, -0.296, -0.014, -0.0395, -0.0128