{"id": "94", "question": "what is the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the main differences between essential and non-essential amino acids are:\n\nEssential Amino Acids:\n\n* Must be obtained from the diet\n* Cannot be produced by the body\n* Nine out of 20 amino acids are considered essential (valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan, and histidine for infants)\n\nNon-Essential Amino Acids:\n\n* Can be produced by the body\n* Not necessarily required to obtain from the diet\n* Include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine\n\nIt is important to note that even though non-essential amino acids can be produced by the body, it is still important to consume a balanced diet that includes all of the essential amino acids to ensure proper bodily functions.", "pred": "Essential amino acids are those that cannot be synthesized by the body and must be obtained from an external source, such as food. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be synthesized by the body and do not need to be obtained from an external source. Essential amino acids are necessary for the proper functioning of the body, while nonessential amino acids can play a variety of roles in the body, but they are not as crucial for survival. Essential amino acids include leucine, isoleucine, valine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan, and histidine. Nonessential amino acids include alanine, aspartic acid, asparagine, arginine, cysteine, glutamic acid, glutamine, glycine, proline, serine, tyrosine, threonine, valine, and tryptophan. Answer: Essential amino acids are those that cannot be synthesized by the body and must be obtained from an external source, such as food. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be synthesized by the body and do not need to be obtained from an external source. Essential amino acids are necessary for the proper functioning of the body, while nonessential amino acids can play a variety of roles in the body, but they are not as crucial for survival. Essential amino acids include leucine, isoleucine, valine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan, and histidine. Nonessential amino acids include alanine, aspartic acid, asparagine, arginine, cysteine, glutamic acid, glutamine, glycine, proline, serine, tyrosine, threonine, valine, and tryptophan. Answer: Essential amino acids are those that cannot be synthesized by the body and must be obtained from an external source, such as food. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be synthesized by the body and do not need to be obtained from an external source. Essential amino acids are necessary for the proper functioning of the body, while nonessential amino acids can play a variety of roles in the body, but they are not as crucial for survival. Essential amino acids include leucine, isoleucine, valine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan, and hist", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_pd", "raw_output": "Answer: Essential amino acids are those that cannot be synthesized by the body and must be obtained from an external source, such as food. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be synthesized by the body and do not need to be obtained from an external source. Essential amino acids are necessary for the proper functioning of the body, while nonessential amino acids can play a variety of roles in the body, but they are not as crucial for survival. Essential amino acids include leucine, isoleucine, valine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan, and histidine. Nonessential amino acids include alanine, aspartic acid, asparagine, arginine, cysteine, glutamic acid, glutamine, glycine, proline, serine, tyrosine, threonine, valine, and tryptophan. Answer: Essential amino acids are those that cannot be synthesized by the body and must be obtained from an external source, such as food. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be synthesized by the body and do not need to be obtained from an external source. Essential amino acids are necessary for the proper functioning of the body, while nonessential amino acids can play a variety of roles in the body, but they are not as crucial for survival. Essential amino acids include leucine, isoleucine, valine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan, and histidine. Nonessential amino acids include alanine, aspartic acid, asparagine, arginine, cysteine, glutamic acid, glutamine, glycine, proline, serine, tyrosine, threonine, valine, and tryptophan. Answer: Essential amino acids are those that cannot be synthesized by the body and must be obtained from an external source, such as food. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be synthesized by the body and do not need to be obtained from an external source. Essential amino acids are necessary for the proper functioning of the body, while nonessential amino acids can play a variety of roles in the body, but they are not as crucial for survival. Essential amino acids include leucine, isoleucine, valine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan, and hist", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\n1. Identify the essential amino acids: These are the amino acids that cannot be synthesized by the body and must be obtained from an external source, such as food.\n2. Identify the nonessential amino acids: These are the amino acids that can be synthesized by the body, so they do not need to be obtained from an external source.\n3. Compare the functions of essential and nonessential amino acids: Essential amino acids are necessary for the proper functioning of the body, while nonessential amino acids can play a variety of roles in the body, but they are not as crucial for survival.\n