{"id": "673", "question": "inventory costing weighted average", "gt": "Sure! Here's my answer to the question:\n\nThe weighted average method of costing inventory assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. When a unit of inventory is sold, the weighted average method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available and assigns that average price to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change.\n\nTo calculate the weighted average cost per unit, divide the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale. The weighted-average figure can then be used to assign costs to both ending inventory and cost of goods sold. The recorded amount of inventory on hand using the weighted average costing method represents a value between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.\n\nWeighted average costing is commonly used in situations where inventory items are intermingled and it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_pd", "raw_output": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nExplain the weighted average inventory costing method and provide an example of its calculation.\nNext logical reasoning step: Provide a detailed explanation of the formula used in the weighted average inventory costing method.\nProvide a detailed explanation of the formula used in the weighted average inventory costing method.\nNext logical reasoning step: Break down the example calculation provided in the passage to illustrate the use of the weighted average formula in inventory costing.\nNext logical reasoning step: Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of using the weighted average inventory costing method relative to other inventory costing methods, such as FIFO and LIFO.\nNext logical reasoning step: Compare the weighted average inventory costing method with other inventory costing methods in terms of accuracy, simplicity, and compliance with accounting standards.\nNext logical reasoning step: Explain how the weighted average inventory costing method can be applied in different industries and scenarios,\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 53.194, "generation_time_seconds": 53.1538, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0341, "record_process": true, "stages": [{"step": 0, "phase": "rollout", "rollouts": [{"beam": 0, "rollout": 0, "token_ids": [198, 16, 13, 64547, 279, 943, 315, 15444, 53724, 1714, 1660, 14078, 624, 17, 13, 70894, 279, 16170, 315, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 624, 18, 13, 1374, 19991, 1246, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 47417, 15444, 7049, 624, 19, 13, 23768, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 448, 1008, 15444, 53724, 5413, 624, 20, 13, 29901, 279, 24154, 315, 1667, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 369, 5896, 12827, 9895, 624, 21, 13, 37427, 2986, 279, 4650, 22146, 323, 63625, 315, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 624, 22, 13, 54115, 279, 5421, 315, 24527, 389, 279, 36824, 5461, 2783, 624, 23, 13, 21144, 279, 14976, 3766, 315, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 304, 2155, 19102, 624, 24, 13, 64547, 894, 3151, 17501, 476, 14305, 8826, 279, 990, 315, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 624, 16, 15], "logp_list": [0.0, -1.1924, -0.0005, -0.5606, -0.0052, -1.4699, -0.0001, -0.0191, -0.0057, -0.0049, -0.3233, -0.0529, -1.747, -0.2179, 0.0, -0.8342, -0.0039, -2.3785, -0.5346, -0.0972, -0.0008, -0.0, -1.1186, -0.0094, -0.0001, 0.0, -2.2405, 0.0, -1.2212, -0.1826, -0.0092, 0.0, -0.0121, -0.2624, -3.1898, -0.0592, -0.0058, -0.0, 0.0, -0.9238, -0.0032, -0.0003, 0.0, -0.0, -0.361, -0.0004, -0.008, -0.0006, -0.0001, -0.2095, -0.0005, 0.0, -3.3063, -0.115, -4.5111, -0.1735, -0.0036, -0.0003, 0.0, 0.0, -0.0, -0.7399, -0.4278, -0.0356, -4.3779, -0.3282, -0.0045, 0.0, -0.6483, -0.0, -0.1373,