{"id": "75", "question": "how to cook brats", "gt": "Based on the passages provided, the steps to cook brats are as follows:\n\n1. Prick the bratwurst with a fork to prevent them from exploding as they cook. \n2. Combine beer and onions in a large pot and bring it to a boil. Submerge the bratwurst in the beer, adding red pepper flakes, garlic powder, salt, and pepper.\n3. Place the pot over medium heat and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes.\n4. In the meantime, preheat an outdoor grill for medium-high heat and lightly oil the grate.\n5. After simmering, remove the bratwurst from the beer mixture and reduce the heat to low, continuing to cook the onions.\n6. Cook the bratwurst on the preheated grill, turning occasionally, for about 10 to 14 minutes until they are browned evenly.\n7. Serve the bratwurst with the beer and onion mixture as a topping or side.", "pred": "To cook brats, first determine the type of bratwurst (e.g., beef, pork, chicken). Preheat the cooking surface to the correct temperature for the chosen cooking method. Prepare the bratwursts by cleaning them and removing any excess casing if necessary. Choose a cooking method, such as cooking on the stove or grill. If cooking on the stove, place the bratwursts in a pot and add water or beer to cover about a quarter of the bratwursts. Bring to a boil and then reduce the heat to a simmer. Cook for about 20-30 minutes, or until the bratwursts are cooked through. Flip the bratwursts occasionally to ensure even cooking.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_pd", "raw_output": "Answer: To cook brats, first determine the type of bratwurst (e.g., beef, pork, chicken). Preheat the cooking surface to the correct temperature for the chosen cooking method. Prepare the bratwursts by cleaning them and removing any excess casing if necessary. Choose a cooking method, such as cooking on the stove or grill. If cooking on the stove, place the bratwursts in a pot and add water or beer to cover about a quarter of the bratwursts. Bring to a boil and then reduce the heat to a simmer. Cook for about 20-30 minutes, or until the bratwursts are cooked through. Flip the bratwursts occasionally to ensure even cooking.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\n1. Determine the type of bratwurst: The type of bratwurst (e.g., beef, pork, chicken) affects the cooking method and timing.\n2. Preheat the cooking surface: Ensure the stove or grill is preheated to the correct temperature for the cooking method.\n3. Prepare the bratwursts: Clean the bratwursts and remove any excess casing if necessary.\n4. Choose a cooking method: Decide whether to cook the bratwursts on the stove, grill, or in the oven.\n5. Cook on the stove: Place the bratwurst\n5. Cook on the stove: Place the bratwursts in a pot on the stove and add water or beer to cover about a quarter of the bratwursts. Bring to a boil and then reduce the heat to a simmer. Cook for about 20-30 minutes, or until the bratwursts are cooked through. Flip the bratwursts occasionally to ensure even cooking.\nCooking on the stove: Place the bratwursts in a pot on the stove and add water or beer to cover about a quarter of the bratwursts. Bring to a boil and then reduce the heat to a simmer. Cook for about 20-30 minutes, or until the bratwursts are cooked through. Flip the bratwursts occasionally to ensure even cooking.\n\nAnswer: To cook brats, first determine the type of bratwurst (e.g., beef, pork, chicken). Preheat the cooking surface to the correct temperature for the chosen cooking method. Prepare the bratwursts by cleaning them and removing any excess casing if necessary. Choose a cooking method, such as cooking on the stove or grill. If cooking on the stove, place the bratwursts in a pot and add water or beer to cover about a quarter of the bratwursts. Bring to a boil and then reduce the heat to a simmer. Cook for about 20-30 minutes, or until the bratwursts are cooked through. Flip the bratwursts occasionally to ensure even cooking.", "time_sec": 24.018, "generation_time_seconds": 23.9768, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0331, "record_process": true, "stages": [{"step": 0, "phase": "rollout", "rollouts": [{"beam": 0, "rollout": 0, "token_ids": [198, 16, 13, 29901, 279, 943, 315, 1411, 266, 86, 32612, 25, 576, 943, 315, 1411, 266, 86, 32612, 320, 68, 1302, 2572, 24317, 11, 35067, 11, 16158, 8, 21501, 279, 17233, 1714, 323, 18405, 624, 17, 13, 4968, 19963, 279, 17233, 7329, 25, 29279, 279, 44015, 476, 39058, 374, 855, 383, 657, 311, 279, 4396, 9315, 369, 279, 17233, 1714, 624, 18, 13, 31166, 279, 1411, 266, 86, 32612, 82, 25, 9590, 279, 1411, 266, 86, 32612, 82, 323, 4057, 894, 13623, 73344, 421, 5871, 624, 19, 13, 22201, 264, 17233, 1714, 25, 98881, 3425, 311, 4296, 279, 1411, 266, 86, 32612, 82, 389, 279, 44015, 11, 39058, 11, 476, 304, 279, 23387, 624, 20, 13, 12514, 389, 279, 44015, 25, 10771, 279, 1411, 266, 86, 32612], "logp_list": [-0.0, -1.2521, -0.0002, -3.964, -0.0388, -0.0542, -0.0055, -0.0006, -1.5019, -0.0264, 0.0, -0.5386, -2.3361, -1.9881, -0.0004, -0.0005, -0.0002, -0.0, 0.0, -0.9652, -0.0426, -0.0, -0.0007, -3.2071, -0.0005, -0.0017, -0.0007, -1.8089, -0.6932, -1.2208, -0.1751, -0.0011, -0.0618, -0.0944, -2.6491, -0.2034, -0.0047, 0.0, -1.3907, -0.0015, -0.0019, -0.2922, -0.433, -0.0002, -0.7163, -0.1839, -2.7754, -0.3065, -0.0082, -0.0002, -0.3443, -0.0, -0.001, -0.0016, -0.0057, -2.0264, -0.0, -0.1416, -0.2832, -0.2193, -0.0001, -0.8912, -0.0, 0.0, -0.0964, -0.0007, -0.0193, -0.5944, -0.0001, -0.0, -1.1943, -0.0002, -1.4678, -0.5973, -0.0005, -0.0055, 0.0, 0.0, -0.004, -0.4191, -0.1541, -0.0793, -0.4633, -1.5926, -0.2719, -0.0924, -0.0169, -0.0, 0.0, -1.7292, -0.8034, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0125, -0.1126, -0.0484, -0.0039, -0.1472, -0.0357, -0.0, -0.2091, 0.0, 0.0, -0.0001, -0.0084, -1.502, -0.1591, -0.0149, -0.1865, -0.0, -0.0045, -0.0256, -0.2267, -0.0, -0.0077, -0.0037, -0.0, -1.1664, -1.3449, -0.0151, -0.0016, -0.077, -1.5629, -0.0017, -0.0003, -0.0001,