{"id": "199", "question": "what causes necrotizing fasciitis", "gt": "Necrotizing fasciitis is caused primarily by bacteria, specifically group A beta-hemolytic streptococci, also known as Streptococcus pyogenes. This is the same bacteria that causes strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. Other bacteria types, such as staphylococcus, are also associated with the disease. The bacteria infect the superficial fascia, a layer of connective tissue below the skin. Prior trauma, such as a cut or scrape, insect bite, burn, or needle puncture wound can serve as a source of infection as well as surgical incision sites and various surgical procedures. In rare cases, fungi can lead to this condition.", "pred": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_pd", "raw_output": "Answer: Unable to answer based on given passages.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nIdentify the key factors or conditions that contribute to necrotizing fasciitis.\nBased on the information provided in the given passage, it is not explicitly stated what causes necrotizing fasciitis. Therefore, I am unable to answer based on the given passages alone.\nUnable to answer based on given passages.\n\nAnswer: Unable to answer based on given passages.", "time_sec": 11.041, "generation_time_seconds": 11.0075, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0269, "record_process": true, "stages": [{"step": 0, "phase": "rollout", "rollouts": [{"beam": 0, "rollout": 0, "token_ids": [198, 785, 32711, 7354, 525, 1447, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0, -1.8987, -0.0431, -0.0006, -0.0, -1.7151, -Infinity, -14.3093, -12.2918, -14.375, -10.4189, -10.3122, -9.22, -9.6067, -8.1413, -3.665, -3.639, -4.6236, -4.4524], "cumulative_logprob": -Infinity, "norm_logp": -Infinity, "input_len": 144, "output_tokens": 19}, {"beam": 0, "rollout": 1, "token_ids": [198, 7