{"id": "360", "question": "What’s the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy?", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is the difference between ad hominem fallacy and poisoning the well fallacy:\n\nAd hominem fallacy refers to attacking an argument by attacking the character, motive, or other attribute of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. Poisoning the well fallacy, on the other hand, is a specific type of ad hominem attack that occurs before the merits of a case can be considered, and it redirects the discussion to the faults of one of the parties.\n\nIn other words, ad hominem fallacy is a broader term that encompasses various types of attacks on the person making the argument, while poisoning the well fallacy is a specific type of ad hominem attack that focuses on the person's integrity or intelligence.", "pred": "The ad hominem fallacy occurs when someone attacks the character or personal attributes of the person making an argument rather than addressing the argument itself. For example, if someone argues that climate change is real and caused by human activity, an opponent might respond by saying \"You must believe in climate change because you're an environmentalist and environmentalists are always wrong.\"", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_pd", "raw_output": "Answer: The ad hominem fallacy occurs when someone attacks the character or personal attributes of the person making an argument rather than addressing the argument itself. For example, if someone argues that climate change is real and caused by human activity, an opponent might respond by saying \"You must believe in climate change because you're an environmentalist and environmentalists are always wrong.\"\n\nThe poisoning the well fallacy occurs when someone tries to undermine an argument by presenting false or misleading information that makes the argument seem flawed or invalid before the argument is even presented. For example, an opponent might say \"You must be lying about the climate change because you just got elected, so you must be a corrupt politician.\" This tries to make the argument seem invalid before it is even made.\n\nThe reasoning steps are:\n\n1. Define the ad hominem fallacy.\n2. Define the poisoning the well fallacy.\n3. Compare the two fallacies by analyzing their characteristics and examples.\n4. Analyze the similarities and differences between the two fallacies.\n5. Provide a clear distinction between the two fallacies.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\nDefine the ad hominem fallacy.\nDefine the poisoning the well fallacy.\nCompare the two fallacies by analyzing their characteristics and examples.\nAnalyze the similarities and differences between the two fallacies.\nProvide a clear distinction between the two fallacies.\nAnswer: The ad hominem fallacy occurs when someone attacks the character or personal attributes of the person making an argument rather than addressing the argument itself. For example, if someone argues that climate change is real and caused by human activity, an opponent might respond by saying \"You must believe in climate change because you're an environmentalist and environmentalists are always wrong.\"\nThe poisoning the well fallacy occurs when someone tries to undermine an argument by presenting false or misleading information that makes the argument seem flawed or invalid before the argument is even presented. For example, an opponent might say \"You must be lying about the climate change because you just got elected, so you must be a corrupt politician.\" This tries to make the argument seem invalid before it is even made.\nThe reasoning steps are:\n\nAnswer: The ad hominem fallacy occurs when someone attacks the character or personal attributes of the person making an argument rather than addressing the argument itself. For example, if someone argues that climate change is real and caused by human activity, an opponent might respond by saying \"You must believe in climate change because you're an environmentalist and environmentalists are always wrong.\"\n\nThe poisoning the well fallacy occurs when someone tries to undermine an argument by presenting false or misleading information that makes the argument seem flawed or invalid before the argument is even presented. For example, an opponent might say \"You must be lying about the climate change because you just got elected, so you must be a corrupt politician.\" This tries to make the argument seem invalid before it is even made.\n\nThe reasoning steps are:\n\n1. Define the ad hominem fallacy.\n2. Define the poisoning the well fallacy.\n3. Compare the two fallacies by analyzing their characteristics and examples.\n4. Analyze the similarities and differences between the two fallacies.\n5. Provide a clear distinction between the two fallacies.", "time_sec": 42.864, "generation_time_seconds": 42.8266, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0312, "record_process": true, "stages": [{"step": 0, "phase": "rollout", "rollouts": [{"beam": 0, "rollout": 0, "token_ids": [198, 785, 32711, 7354, 525, 24391, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645], "logp_list": [0.0, -0.7606, -0.0085, -0.0007, -0.0002, -0.277, -8.4598, -15.8567, -10.6255, -8.9585, -0.1733, -4.4221, -3.4636, -5.0711, -2.1046, -0.1223, -0.0332, -0.0615, -0.3986, -0.2748, -0.1769, -0.1131, -0.0949, -0.0525, -0.3249, -1.21, -1.8456, -1.0574, -0.1183, -0.0654, -0.4314, -2.2046, -4.3892, -4.1841, -2.2034, -0.597, -0.3636, -1.1995, -2.2027, -3.3703, -1.1974, -0.5092, -0.1745, -0.2111, -0.5971, -1.3471, -1.6732, -1.0562, -0.9255, -0.9248, -1.8424, -2.9706, -1.6691, -0.9249, -0.5085, -0.2537, -0.2536, -0.211, -0.2532, -0.2104, -0.2101, -0.3625, -0.9257, -3.9809, -3.1746, -3.9793, -2.7814, -0.2626, -0.0312, -0.0301, -0.2546, -0.8062, -1.057, -0.6973, -0.6966, -2.7745, -3.7749, -3.7744, -1.504, -1.0558, -1.1963, -1.5036, -2.2013, -1.8401, -1.6679, -1.1951, -1.0549, -1.8407, -3.5706, -5.0068, -3.5704, -3.3688, -2.5796, -2.3887, -2.7734, -5.0068, -4.5936, -3.7735, -1.8404, -0.9247, -1.1958, -2.3886, -2.7734, -3.7736, -3.9775, -2.9699, -2.7733, -3.5705, -4.5936, -5.0068, -5.4211, -5.2139, -3.3687, -2.0184, -1.668, -1.3443, -1.0542, -0.3616, -0.6945, -1.1959, -1.056, -2.019, -2.0187, -1.8412], "cumulative_logprob": -251.3203, "norm_logp": -2.0268, "input_len": 153, "output_tokens": 124}, {"beam": 0, "rollout": 1, "token_ids": [198, 35338, 279, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 624, 35338, 279, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 624, 27374, 279, 1378, 4399, 26030, 553, 41018, 862, 17452, 323, 10295, 624, 2082, 55856, 279, 42774, 323, 11799, 1948, 279, 1378, 4399, 26030, 624, 60424, 264, 2797, 29196, 1948, 279, 1378, 4399, 26030, 624, 16141, 25, 576, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 13666, 979, 4325, 8776, 279, 3668, 476, 4345, 8201, 315, 279, 1697, 3259, 458, 5693, 4751, 1091, 27020, 279, 5693, 5086, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 421, 4325, 28533, 429, 9977, 2297, 374, 1931, 323, 8881, 553, 3738, 5702, 11, 458, 14704, 2578, 5889, 553, 5488, 330, 261