{"id": "212", "question": "differences between elements compounds and mixtures", "gt": "Here are my answers to the question based on the provided passages:\n\nQ: What are the differences between elements, compounds, and mixtures?\nA: Based on the passages provided, here are the differences between elements, compounds, and mixtures:\n\nElements:\n\n* Are the purest form of a chemical substance (Passage 3)\n* Consist of only one type of atom with the same number of protons (Passage 3)\n\nCompounds:\n\n* Are substances composed of two or more different elements (Passage 3)\n* Are also pure substances (Passage 3)\n\nMixtures:\n\n* Contain two or more substances that are relatively easy to separate (Passage 1)\n* Have a variable composition (Passage 2)\n* Show the properties of their constituent substances (Passage 2)\n* Can be separated by physical means (Passage 2)", "pred": "Elements, compounds, and mixtures differ in their composition, properties, and how they are formed. Elements are pure substances consisting of only one type of atom. For example, oxygen (O2) and carbon (C) are elements. Compounds are substances made up of two or more different types of atoms chemically bonded together. For example, water (H2O) and carbon dioxide (CO2) are compounds. Mixtures are combinations of two or more substances that are not chemically bonded. For example, air is a mixture of nitrogen, oxygen, and other gases. Elements, compounds, and mixtures differ in their composition, properties, and how they are formed. Elements are pure substances consisting of only one type of atom. For example, oxygen (O2) and carbon (C) are elements. Compounds are substances made up of two or more different types of atoms chemically bonded together. For example, water (H2O) and carbon dioxide (CO2) are compounds. Mixtures are combinations of two or more substances that are not chemically bonded. For example, air is a mixture of nitrogen, oxygen, and other gases. Elements, compounds, and mixtures diff