 between elements, compounds, and mixtures.\nThe reasoning steps are:\nThe reasoning steps are:\n\nAnswer: Elements, compounds, and mixtures differ in their composition, properties, and how they are formed. Elements are pure substances consisting of only one type of atom. For example, oxygen (O2) and carbon (C) are elements. Compounds are substances made up of two or more different types of atoms chemically bonded together. For example, water (H2O) and carbon dioxide (CO2) are compounds. Mixtures are combinations of two or more substances that are not chemically bonded. For example, air is a mixture of nitrogen, oxygen, and other gases. Elements, compounds, and mixtures differ in their composition, properties, and how they are formed. Elements are pure substances consisting of only one type of atom. For example, oxygen (O2) and carbon (C) are elements. Compounds are substances made up of two or more different types of atoms chemically bonded together. For example, water (H2O) and carbon dioxide (CO2) are compounds. Mixtures are combinations of two or more substances that are not chemically bonded. For example, air is a mixture of nitrogen, oxygen, and other gases. Elements, compounds, and mixtures differ in their composition, properties, and how they are formed. Elements are pure substances consisting of only one type of atom. For example, oxygen (O2) and carbon (C) are elements. Compounds are substances made up of two or more different types of atoms chemically bonded together. For example, water (H2O) and carbon dioxide (CO2) are compounds. Mixtures are combinations of two or more substances that are not chemically bonded. For example, air is a mixture of nitrogen, oxygen, and other gases. Elements, compounds, and mixtures differ in their composition, properties, and how they are formed. Elements are pure substances consisting of only one type of atom. For example, oxygen (O2) and carbon (C) are elements. Compounds are substances made up of two or more different t