{"id": "295", "question": "what is the proper way to fertilize your grass", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the proper way to fertilize your grass is to:\n\n1. Fertilize at the right time of year, which is at the beginning of the growing season, and varies for different types of grasses.\n2. Choose a fertilizer that is suitable for your type of grass and soil pH, as determined by a soil test.\n3. Apply the fertilizer in a perpendicular pattern, avoiding over-fertilization, which can be determined by checking if the leaf blades bounce back up after being stepped on.", "pred": "The proper way to fertilize your grass involves several steps. First, identify the type of grass in your yard. Different grass types have different nutrient requirements. For example, Kentucky bluegrass requires more nitrogen than fescue. Next, determine the soil type in your yard. Sandy soils may require more phosphorus and potassium than clay soils. Choose the appropriate fertilizer for your grass type and soil. For instance, a fertilizer with a ratio of 10-10-10 is suitable for most grass types. Follow the instructions on the fertilizer packaging for application. This includes the amount to use and the frequency of application. Apply the fertilizer at the recommended time of year, which is typically in the spring or fall. Finally, water the grass after application to help the fertilizer penetrate the soil. This ensures that the nutrients are available to the grass roots. By following these steps, you can ensure that your grass receives the proper nutrients for healthy growth. Answer:[The proper way to fertilize your grass involves several steps. First, identify the type of grass in your yard. Different grass types have different nutrient requirements. For example, Kentucky bluegrass requires more nitrogen than fescue. Next, determine the soil type in your yard. Sandy soils may require more phosphorus and potassium than clay soils. Choose the appropriate fertilizer for your grass type and soil. For instance, a fertilizer with a ratio of 10-10-10 is suitable for most grass types. Follow the instructions on the fertilizer packaging for application. This includes the amount to use and the frequency of application. Apply the fertilizer at the recommended time of year, which is typically in the spring or fall. Finally, water the grass after application to help the fertilizer penetrate the soil. This ensures that the nutrients are available to the grass roots. By following these steps, you can ensure that your grass receives the proper nutrients for healthy growth.]", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_pd", "raw_output": "Answer: The proper way to fertilize your grass involves several steps. First, identify the type of grass in your yard. Different grass types have different nutrient requirements. For example, Kentucky bluegrass requires more nitrogen than fescue. Next, determine the soil type in your yard. Sandy soils may require more phosphorus and potassium than clay soils. Choose the appropriate fertilizer for your grass type and soil. For instance, a fertilizer with a ratio of 10-10-10 is suitable for most grass types. Follow the instructions on the fertilizer packaging for application. This includes the amount to use and the frequency of application. Apply the fertilizer at the recommended time of year, which is typically in the spring or fall. Finally, water the grass after application to help the fertilizer penetrate the soil. This ensures that the nutrients are available to the grass roots. By following these steps, you can ensure that your grass receives the proper nutrients for healthy growth. Answer:[The proper way to fertilize your grass involves several steps. First, identify the type of grass in your yard. Different grass types have different nutrient requirements. For example, Kentucky bluegrass requires more nitrogen than fescue. Next, determine the soil type in your yard. Sandy soils may require more phosphorus and potassium than clay soils. Choose the appropriate fertilizer for your grass type and soil. For instance, a fertilizer with a ratio of 10-10-10 is suitable for most grass types. Follow the instructions on the fertilizer packaging for application. This includes the amount to use and the frequency of application. Apply the fertilizer at the recommended time of year, which is typically in the spring or fall. Finally, water the grass after application to help the fertilizer penetrate the soil. This ensures that the nutrients are available to the grass roots. By following these steps, you can ensure that your grass receives the proper nutrients for healthy growth.]", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\n1. Identify the type of grass in your yard.\n2. Determine the soil type in your yard.\n3. Choose the appropriate fertilizer for your grass type and soil.\n4. Follow the instructions on the fertilizer packaging for application.\n5. Apply the fertilizer at the recommended time of year.\n6. Water the grass after application to help the fertilizer penetrate the soil.\nThe reasoning steps are:\nThe reasoning steps are:\n\nAnswer: The proper way to fertilize your grass involves several steps. First, identify the type of grass in your yard. Different grass types have different nutrient requirements. For example, Kentucky bluegrass requires more nitrogen than fescue. Next, determine the soil type in your yard. Sandy soils may require more phosphorus and potassium than clay soils. Choose the appropriate fertilizer for your grass type and soil. For instance, a fertilizer with a ratio of 10-10-10 is suitable for most grass types. Follow the instructions on the fertilizer packaging for application. This includes the amount to use and the frequency of application. Apply the fertilizer at the recommended time of year, which is typically in the spring or fall. Finally, water the grass after application to help the fertilizer penetrate the soil. This ensures that the nutrients are available to the grass roots. By following these steps, you can ensure that your grass receives the proper nutrients for healthy growth. Answer:[The proper way to fertilize your grass involves several steps. First, identify the type of grass in your yard. Different grass types have different nutrient requirements. For example, Kentucky bluegrass requires more nitrogen than fescue. Next, determine the soil type in your yard. Sandy soils may require more phosphorus and potassium than clay soils. Choose the appropriate fertilizer for your grass type and soil. For instance, a fertilizer with a ratio of 10-10-10 is suitable for most grass types. Follow the instructions on the fertilizer packaging for application. This includes the amount to use and the frequency of application. Apply the fertilizer at the recommended time of year, which is typically in the spring or fall. Finally, water the grass after application to help the fertilizer penetrate the soil. This ensures that the nutrients are available to the grass roots. By following these steps, you can ensure that your grass receives the proper nutrients for healthy growth.]", "time_sec": 25.595, "generation_time_seconds": 25.5543, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0345, "record_process": true, "stages": [{"step": 0, "phase": "rollout", "rollouts": [{"beam": 0, "rollout": 0, "token_ids": [198, 17075, 311, 4226, 3118, 389, 2661, 46769, 13, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0, -2.0115, 0.0, -0.0001