{"id": "411", "question": "how to make panko crumbs", "gt": "Sure, I can help you with that! Based on the given passages, here's a step-by-step guide on how to make panko crumbs:\n\n1. Preheat the oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C).\n2. Push torn pieces of bread or chunks of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to make coarse crumbs.\n3. Spread the crumbs on a baking sheet.\n4. Bake in preheated oven until crumbs are dry but not toasted, 6 to 8 minutes. Shake the sheet twice during baking to evenly dry the crumbs.\n5. Remove from the oven and cool completely.\n\nIf you want to store the panko crumbs for later use, you can transfer them to an airtight container and keep them at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you have any further questions.", "pred": "To make panko crumbs, you need plain bread, an oven or dehydrator, and a food processor or a rolling pin. Cut the bread into slices about 1/2 inch thick. Crush the bread slices into coarse crumbs using a food processor or a rolling pin. Dry the bread crumbs in an oven or dehydrator set to a low temperature (around 200°F - 250°F) for about 30 minutes to an hour. This process helps to remove excess moisture and gives the panko crumbs a crisp texture.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_pd", "raw_output": "Answer: To make panko crumbs, you need plain bread, an oven or dehydrator, and a food processor or a rolling pin. Cut the bread into slices about 1/2 inch thick. Crush the bread slices into coarse crumbs using a food processor or a rolling pin. Dry the bread crumbs in an oven or dehydrator set to a low temperature (around 200°F - 250°F) for about 30 minutes to an hour. This process helps to remove excess moisture and gives the panko crumbs a crisp texture.", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\n1. Identify the type of breading: Panko crumbs are a type of Japanese breadcrumbs, which are coarser and have a distinct texture compared to regular breadcrumbs. This texture makes them ideal for breading and frying.\n2. Gather ingredients: To make panko crumbs, you will need plain bread, an oven or dehydrator, and a food processor or a rolling pin.\n3. Prepare the bread: Cut the bread into slices, but do not cut them too thin. The thickness depends on the desired texture of the panko crumbs. Typically, slices that are about 1/2 inch thick are used.\n4.\n- **Crush the bread slices**: Place the bread slices in a food processor or use a rolling pin to crush them into coarse crumbs. The goal is to achieve a texture similar to panko, which is coarser than regular breadcrumbs but finer than bread crumbs.\n- **Dry the bread crumbs**: Place the crushed bread crumbs in an oven or a dehydrator set to a low temperature (around 200°F - 250°F) to dry them out. This step is crucial as it helps to remove excess moisture and gives the panko crumbs a crisp texture. It typically takes about 30 minutes to an hour, depending on the thickness of the bread slices and the size of the crumbs.\n\nAnswer: To make panko crumbs, you need plain bread, an oven or dehydrator, and a food processor or a rolling pin. Cut the bread into slices about 1/2 inch thick. Crush the bread slices into coarse crumbs using a food processor or a rolling pin. Dry the bread crumbs in an oven or dehydrator set to a low temperature (around 200°F - 250°F) for about 30 minutes to an hour. This process helps to remove excess moisture and gives the panko crumbs a crisp texture.", "time_sec": 21.857, "generation_time_seconds": 21.8106, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0386, "record_process": true, "stages": [{"step": 0, "phase": "rollout", "rollouts": [{"beam": 0, "rollout": 0, "token_ids": [198, 16, 13, 64547, 279, 13966, 2567, 369, 3259, 281, 92852, 96577, 624, 17, 13, 29901, 279, 1882, 315, 20045, 279, 13966, 624, 18, 13, 51909, 279, 7354, 369, 26792, 323, 45520, 279, 281, 92852, 96577, 624, 19, 13, 39565, 10414, 476, 26244, 3