, -0.0056, -0.0, -0.2527, -0.0, -0.5836, -0.0715, -0.0733, -1.8153, -0.0432, -0.0556, -0.0553, -0.0124, -0.0, -0.0016, -0.5388, -0.0071, -0.0343, -0.3234, -0.4047, -0.7127, -0.7028, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0025, -0.33, -0.1228, -0.5435, -0.0011, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0021, -0.1944, -0.1067, -0.0485, -0.6869, -0.3328, -0.0016, -0.6182, -0.3036, -0.2436, -0.9672, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.1052, -0.0295, -0.0712, -0.0052, -0.0349, -0.5161, -0.0003, -0.1459, -0.4341, -0.0057, -0.7327, -0.0478, -0.0326, -0.3959, -0.0764, -0.0093, -0.0645, -0.1694, -0.7481, -0.7906, -0.2236, -0.1182, -0.0076, -0.9704, -0.1132, -0.0, -0.0732, -0.3366, -0.3515, -0.2018, -0.1632, -0.1329, -0.6533, -0.3337, -0.6326, -0.1435, -1.0691, -0.0946, -0.0741, -0.0003, -0.3066, -0.0, -0.0107, -0.0001, -0.0008, -0.51, -0.0049, -0.2307, -0.7558, -0.6668, -0.002, -0.002, -0.5647, -0.0311, -0.576, -0.3345, -0.0, -0.4817, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0732, -0.0413, -0.4057, -0.0874, -0.003, -0.0294, -0.5682, -0.0124, -0.0018, -0.0, -0.053, -0.0013, -0.308, -0.1648, -0.0, -0.0401, -0.0001, -0.0005, -0.0041, -0.0703, -0.195, -0.0217, -0.2491, -0.0676, -0.2238, -0.0, -0.2837, -0.0001, -0.0005, -0.3649, -0.0085, -0.7798, -0.8457, -0.0293, -0.0227, -0.1824, -1.0549, -0.5426, -0.0, -0.0019, -0.0, -0.1506, -0.0846, -0.509, -0.083, -0.0, -0.093, -0.0003, -0.0069, -0.193, -0.1506, -0.0002, -0.0111, -0.6209], "cumulative_logprob": -55.9985, "norm_logp": -0.2569, "input_len": 232, "output_tokens": 218}, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 28.57, "GAVIE-Rel": 7.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "647", "question": "how to plant a potato that has sprouted", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are the steps to plant a potato that has sprouted:\n\n1. Cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each, making sure to not break the long sprouts.\n2. Allow the cut ends to dry out overnight in a cool, dark location.\n3. Plant the potato chunks or whole potatoes in well-draining soil, spacing them 24 to 36 inches apart.\n4. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep, and place the potato pieces in the bottom of the trench with the sprouted side facing up.\n5. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\n\nNote that the passages do not provide specific information on how to care for the potatoes after planting, such as watering and fertilization.", "pred": "To plant a sprouted potato, follow these steps:", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_pd", "raw_output": "Answer: To plant a sprouted potato, follow these steps:\n\n1. Identify the sprouted potato.\n2. Select a suitable planting location, such as a garden with well-draining soil and partial sunlight.\n3. Prepare the soil by loosening it and removing any weeds or debris.\n4. Create a trench about 6-8 inches deep and 12-18 inches wide.\n5. Place the sprouted potato in the trench with the sprout facing upward.\n6. Cover the potato with soil, leaving about 1-2 inches of the sprout exposed.\n7. Water the potato gently to moisten the soil without causing waterlogging.\n8. Continue to monitor the potato for growth and adjust the soil as needed to ensure it remains moist but not waterlogged. Regularly check for signs of disease or pests and take appropriate action to address any issues. As the potato grows, you may need to add more soil to the trench to keep the sprout covered and protected. Finally, once the potato has fully grown and is ready to harvest, carefully dig it up from the soil, being careful not to damage the tuber. Enjoy your homegrown potatoes!", "cot": "The reasoning steps are:\n\n1. Identify the sprouted potato.\n2. Select a suitable planting location, such as a garden with well-draining soil and partial sunlight.\n3. Prepare the soil by loosening it and removing any weeds or debris.\n4. Create a trench about 6-8 inches deep and 12-18 inches wide.\n5. Place the sprouted potato in the trench with the sprout facing upward.\n6. Cover the potato with soil, leaving about 1-2 inches of the sprout exposed.\n7. Water the potato gently to moisten the soil without causing waterlogging.\n8. Continue to monitor the\nThe reasoning steps are:\nThe reasoning steps are:\n\nAnswer: To plant a sprouted potato, follow these steps:\n\n1. Identify the sprouted potato.\n2. Select a suitable planting location, such as a garden with well-draining soil and partial sunlight.\n3. Prepare the soil by loosening it and removing any weeds or debris.\n4. Create a trench about 6-8 inches deep and 12-18 inches wide.\n5. Place the sprouted potato in the trench with the sprout facing upward.\n6. Cover the potato with soil, leaving about 1-2 inches of the sprout exposed.\n7. Water the potato gently to moisten the soil without causing waterlogging.\n8. Continue to monitor the potato for growth and adjust the soil as needed to ensure it remains moist but not waterlogged. Regularly check for signs of disease or pests and take appropriate action to address any issues. As the potato grows, you may need to add more soil to the trench to keep the sprout covered and protected. Finally, once the potato has fully grown and is ready to harvest, carefully dig it up from the soil, being careful not to damage the tuber. Enjoy your homegrown potatoes!", "time_sec": 42.679, "generation_time_seconds": 42.6334, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0362, "record_process": true, "stages": [{"step": 0, "phase": "rollout", "rollouts": [{"beam": 0, "rollout": 0, "token_ids": [198, 16, 13, 64